      {
        "narration": "A wave in Alaska tore out trees 524 metres up. That is higher than any building standing on Earth today.",
        "visual_query": "alaskan fjord steep forested slopes",
        "visual_fallback": "alaskan fjord"
      },
      {
        "narration": "On the ninth of July, 1958, the Fairweather Fault slipped, somewhere between magnitude seven point eight and eight point three.",
        "visual_query": "alaska coastal mountains glacier ice",
        "visual_fallback": "coastal mountains"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It shook loose the head of a narrow inlet. Thirty million cubic metres of rock dropped into deep water at once.",
        "visual_query": "rockslide cliff face falling debris",
        "visual_fallback": "rock cliff"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In a fjord that narrow the water cannot spread. It climbed the opposite slope and sheared off mature forest at a clean line.",
        "visual_query": "bare treeline scar mountain slope",
        "visual_fallback": "bare treeline"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Fishing boats were anchored inside. One was carried over the treetops; both aboard lived to describe it. On another boat, two died.",
